<section class="section charlotte-problems" id="common-problems-charlotte">
    <div class="container">
        <div class="section-heading">
            <span class="eyebrow">CHARLOTTE HOMEOWNER GUIDE</span>
            <h2>The 5 most common water heater problems in Charlotte.</h2>
            <p>Hard water, older tanks and the Charlotte-area climate create a specific set of failure patterns. Here is what we see the most — and when it is time to stop troubleshooting and call a professional.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="charlotte-problems-grid">
            <article class="charlotte-problem-card">
                <span>01</span>
                <h3>No hot water or only lukewarm water</h3>
                <p>On electric units, the most common cause is a burned-out heating element or a failed thermostat. On gas units, it is usually a faulty thermocouple, pilot-light or gas-control problem.</p>
                <em>When to call a professional: Call a professional if resetting the breaker or relighting the pilot does not restore hot water within one heating cycle.</em>
            </article>
            <article class="charlotte-problem-card">
                <span>02</span>
                <h3>Popping, rumbling or whistling noises</h3>
                <p>Almost always sediment build-up on the bottom of the tank — very common across the Charlotte metro area because of hard water. The sediment traps steam, which pops as it escapes.</p>
                <em>When to call a professional: Schedule a professional inspection before the noise turns into recurrent overheating or a stuck relief valve.</em>
            </article>
            <article class="charlotte-problem-card">
                <span>03</span>
                <h3>Water pooling around the tank</h3>
                <p>Could be the temperature-and-pressure relief valve, a loose connection, or the tank shell corroding from the inside out. A tank-shell leak is not repairable — the unit must be replaced.</p>
                <em>When to call a professional: Shut off the water supply and call SAFE the same day. Water on electrical parts is a safety hazard.</em>
            </article>
            <article class="charlotte-problem-card">
                <span>04</span>
                <h3>Rust-colored water or a rotten-egg smell</h3>
                <p>Indicates internal tank corrosion (the sacrificial anode rod is spent) or bacterial growth if the heater was off for weeks. Both progress quickly if ignored.</p>
                <em>When to call a professional: Compare the hot- and cold-water sides. If only the hot side is affected, call a professional to inspect the anode and tank condition.</em>
            </article>
            <article class="charlotte-problem-card">
                <span>05</span>
                <h3>Water heater older than 10-15 years</h3>
                <p>Many Charlotte-area homes still run water heaters past their designed service life, which sharply raises the risk of a sudden tank failure and flood damage.</p>
                <em>When to call a professional: Book a preventive assessment — replacing a working old unit on your schedule is always cheaper than an emergency call after a tank ruptures.</em>
            </article>
        </div>
        <div class="hero-actions" style="margin-top:28px">
            <a href="/water-heater-leaking/" class="button button-red">Leaking tank</a>
            <a href="/no-hot-water/" class="button button-outline">No hot water</a>
            <a href="/water-heater-problems" class="button button-outline">Full problem guide</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

